<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romani people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Roma, Romani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Romani people, or Roma, are an ethnic group whose ancestors left north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around a thousand years ago and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the 14th century. Long subject to discrimination, enslavement in Wallachia and Moldavia, and expulsions, they were targeted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and its allies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when hundreds of thousands were murdered. They are Europe's largest ethnic minority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romani-people.jpg" alt="Romani people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AdiJapan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Romani_people.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Linguistic and genetic evidence suggests that the Romani originated in north-western regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, particularly in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. Because the Romani did not chronicle their history, most hypotheses about early Roma rest on linguistic data. Genetic findings published in 2012 suggest that the ancestors of present-day scheduled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and scheduled tribe populations of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, traditionally called the Doma, are the likely ancestral populations of European Roma.</p><p>Historians now believe the westward migration, possibly in waves, occurred around 1000 CE, perhaps in the context of the raids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>. The Romani moved west into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Armenian-origin words in Romani indicate that they passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Romani arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, probably in the twelfth century. A document of 1068 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> mentions Atsingani, probably meaning Roma. The English word Gypsy comes from Egipcien, the medieval belief that they were itinerant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. Their reception was mixed. The first recorded transaction for a Romani slave in Wallachia dates to 1385, while Holy Roman Emperor Sigismund issued them safe conduct in 1417.</p><p>Expulsions followed: from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1416, Lucerne in 1471, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1493, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1504, Catalonia in 1512, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1525, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1530 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1536. From 1510 any Roma found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> were to be executed, and Portugal began deporting Roma to its colonies in 1538. Large-scale emigration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> began in the 1860s. In the mid- to late twentieth century forced sterilisation in several European countries reduced Roma populations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Romani traditionally led a nomadic, itinerant life. Many subgroups and clans name themselves, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinti">Sinti</a>, Kalderash, Lovari and Manouche. The Romani language is Indo-Aryan with strong Persian, Armenian, Greek and South Slavic influence, and its dialects have more than 2 million speakers. Romanipen is the totality of the Romani spirit, culture, law and identity. The first World Romani Congress in 1971 voted to reject all exonyms for the Romani, including Gypsy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Romani people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1992 census counted 10,394,026 inhabitants: Serbs 62.6%, Albanians 16.5% and Montenegrins 5%, with Hungarians, Yugoslavs, Muslims, <b>Romani</b>, Croats, Bulgarians and others.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic inherited a landlocked country in the Carpathian Basin, on the Danube and the Tisza, with some ten million people, mostly Magyars with a large <b>Romani</b> minority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Businesses owned by Jews and Serbs were placed under state commissars, and on 10 December 1941 the postal bank accounts of Serbs, Jews and <b>Roma</b> were confiscated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1941 <b>Romani</b> men were forced into the Labour Service battalions of the Honvéd, where, the historian Katalin Katz noted, they were at the mercy of merciless and often sadistic officers and many died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1941 the Ustaše state and the occupation regimes turned these divisions into mass murder of Serbs, Jews and <b>Roma</b>, notably at the Jasenovac camp, and civil war between Chetniks and Partisans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…by the Nuremberg Laws of September 1935 and terrorised in the November pogrom of 1938, after which their businesses were confiscated; <b>Roma</b> and Sinti, homosexual men and people labelled ‘asocial’ were also persecuted, and about 70,000 disabled patients were murdered in the Aktion T4 programme by August 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
